    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. and regarding the insurance part, the contract says the seller only needs to provide minimum cover. does the buyer have any recourse if something goes wrong during transit, or is the seller's job pretty much done once the goods are on the vessel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF Incoterms 2020, the seller is indeed responsible for providing minimum cover insurance for the goods during transit. However, the buyer has the option to purchase additional insurance to cover more risks, just like a savvy traveler might buy extra travel insurance for peace of mind. If something goes wrong during transit and the minimum cover isn't sufficient, the buyer may have recourse through the insurance company, provided they have purchased additional coverage. It's like having a safety net beneath your feet; the seller provides the basic net, but the buyer can choose to add more layers for extra security. So, while the seller's job isn't entirely done once the goods are on the vessel, the buyer has the opportunity to step up their own protection if they wish. Just remember, with more insurance, you might have to pay a little extra, but it's like paying a small fee for a sturdy, well-built fortress to keep your precious cargo safe.",
